<p>The Emerson Institute was Mandan's second public building, following the log-cabin county courthouse. It originated with “a debating society which was conducted for the mental recreation and improvement of the first settlers of the town.” The group approached the Northern Pacific Railway, which offered three lots for “reasonable consideration” on the condition that they be used for a lecture and education center. The institute honored writer and philosopher Ralph Waldo Emerson, who died in April 1882.</p>

<p>Frank Mead, Michael Lang, B. L. Winston, and P. O. Chilstrom funded the initial land purchase. H. R. Lyon, Frank Farnsworth, James Bellows, Carpenter &amp; Cary, George Bingenheimer, and E. F. Doran also helped establish the venue. Community donations raised {% inflation 3000, 1882 %} to construct the original 36-by-80-foot wooden building, completed in fall 1882, only three years after Mandan's founding.</p>

<p>The building was described as containing modern improvements and tasteful finishes. It had a ticket office on one side and a waiting room and women's dressing room on the other. Although traveling professionals occasionally used the theater, Mandan's dramatic club presented most performances.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/emerson-institute-opera-house/371-expanded-first-school.jpg" alt="Expanded Emerson Institute and Opera House">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The 1884 <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> immigrant edition highlighted the venue as evidence of the cultural and social life of a three-year-old city of 2,500 people. Its eastern stage was described as large enough for ordinary productions and equipped with scenery, footlights, and the accessories of a first-class proscenium. Tuttle and Wilson were the original managers; H. R. Lyon held the position by 1884.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/emerson-institute-opera-house/401-opera-house.jpg" alt="Mandan Opera House around 1906">
    <figcaption>Opera House, circa 1906</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>National theatrical guides listed a 30-by-40-foot stage, a proscenium 20 feet wide and 12 feet high, and seating for 300. Chairs could be rented through Bingenheimer &amp; Taylor's Drug Store, while a piano and scenery were available on-site. The building was featured in Mandan's 1906 promotional souvenir book.</p>

<p>The institute hosted Independence Day and New Year's Eve dances, drama, vaudeville, basketball games, graduations, and public meetings. An addition completed on the east side in spring 1893 enlarged the stage and backstage areas and raised seating capacity to 500. The expanded venue was renamed the Opera House.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/emerson-institute-opera-house/708-1906-new-years-dance-untouched.jpg" alt="New Year's Eve library-benefit dance at the Mandan Opera House">
    <figcaption>New Year's Eve library-benefit dance at the Mandan Opera House, 1903</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Dances were frequent fundraisers in the early 1900s. The Ladies of the Library board held an annual New Year's Eve dance at the Opera House, often accompanied by the seven-piece Lochner's Orchestra. Supper was served onstage at 11 p.m. Dorothy Fiske served as board president.</p>

<p>The Opera House accommodated a wide range of social and cultural events from the 1880s through the 1920s, including professional wrestling and boxing. The Mandan Elks held their first meetings there in 1911. High school athletics moved to the school gymnasium after it opened in 1919. Live entertainment declined after the {% place "palace-theatre" %}Palace Theatre{% endplace %} opened in December 1916.</p>

<p>The building also served as Mandan's first North Dakota National Guard armory. After the {% place "mandan-memorial-building" %}World War Memorial Building{% endplace %}, now Mandan City Hall, opened in 1934, the Opera House was no longer needed. It was demolished and the lot sold.</p>
